{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030343-status-police-and-usa-sheriffs","id":1030343,"title":"Status ( Police ) and USA Sheriffs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-11T17:28:56-08:00","id":1030343,"title":"Status ( Police ) and USA Sheriffs","content":"Sheriffs in USA are limited law enforcement leaders on very small units. So by the Status dots system they should be like Status (Police) 5 for local town - as they are the peak of local law enforcement. But this does not sound right, somehow to me. On first thought I would rather point Sheriff as something like Status (Police) 3 figure.<br /> <br /> Also, deputy sheriffs are about as 'uniforms' in larger cities - i.e. Status (Police) 1, am I getting it right?<br /> <br /> And also do not 'bigger counties' - with proper precincts and Police Chiefs - are over jurisdiction on local Sheriffs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T19:31:55-08:00","id":1030392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030343-status-police-and-usa-sheriffs#post1030343\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sheriffs in USA are limited law enforcement leaders on very small units.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're not necessarily small units, and while limited in some places, they have full authority of the law in most. It's going to depend a lot on the state.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So by the Status dots system they should be like Status (Police) 5 for local town - as they are the peak of local law enforcement. But this does not sound right, somehow to me. On first thought I would rather point Sheriff as something like Status (Police) 3 figure. </div> </div> </div>The sheriff of a small town would be an elected official, and the highest ranking law enforcement officer in the county, so he'd certainly be more well known than 3, and his authority would certainly justify a 5. In a small town the only one who would vie with the sheriff would be a mayor, not counting unique cases. It's also worth noting that, as an elected official, the sheriff can't normally just be fired. A chief of police or police commissioner, both appointed positions, could be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, deputy sheriffs are about as 'uniforms' in larger cities - i.e. Status (Police) 1, am I getting it right? </div> </div> </div>Yes. The sheriff's deputy would usually have a larger area of jurisdiction. Police jurisdiction lines tend to be pretty small, while a sheriff's department watches a whole county, sometimes a state.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And also do not 'bigger counties' - with proper precincts and Police Chiefs - are over jurisdiction on local Sheriffs? </div> </div> </div>Not really. It's going to depend on the situation, and the place.<br /> <br /> You should check out Tales from the 13th Precinct, I'm pretty sure it gets into this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T23:20:55-08:00","id":1030451,"title":"","content":"I don't use American scenes so often, but have same problem. Does not we should be guided by Staff Merit? Citizen can be arrested (quilted or not) by sherif or his deputy. The presence of large authority will not save him if Sherif did it abusively. I.e. I want to say, from outside the sheriff and his deputy will look the same. The status will affect their powers within the organization (in this case the local police) For example, all of the city management fits in 3 dots Staff Merit. Third dot in Status merit it's mayor. Second - is service chiefs (including sherif). First dot it's their deputy and other grass-roots workers. And how many authority sherif of two thousands town have in NY? Only on two dots. Or nothing at all. And in his town two dots will be enough - people who know him for fifteen years will not take him seriously anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12029,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12029-osuneko","user":"OsuNeko","date":". 01-12-2017, 02:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12029-osuneko","name":"OsuNeko","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T02:56:33-08:00","id":1030491,"title":"","content":"Status describes your affiliation with a certain organization or social structure. This has however not to be an official existing group like a police department but can be ajusted to reflect actual dependencies. For a small town, let's call it Springfield, I would advice to not define the organization as &quot;Springfield police&quot; but rather broaden it to something like &quot;Springfield authorities&quot;. This could look something like this:<br /> 1 dot<br /> Lower employees of the town: gardeners, administration workers, tourist information guides etc<br /> 2 dots<br /> Sheriff deputies, secretary of the mayor<br /> 3 dots<br /> Higher administrative staff, like the treasurer or something close<br /> 4 dots<br /> Sheriff, members of the city council<br /> 5 dots<br /> Mayor<br /> <br /> Note that the 1 dot merrit usually doesn't give you any form of authority in the given organization. On the contrary it makes you easier to be pursuaded by persons in that given organization with a higher status. You do get the benefit of access to facilities and of being known, though. Therefore the example 1 dot police status from the core book lists &quot;paid informant&quot; and not the lowest actual police officer.<br /> If you do want to limit the status only to the sheriffs department, I would go something like this:<br /> <br /> 1 dot<br /> Secretary, cleaning personal<br /> 2 dots<br /> Deputies<br /> 3 dots<br /> -<br /> 4 dots<br /> Sheriff<br /> 5 dots<br /> -<br /> You don't have to use all the dots of the merit. Since the sheriff solely decides on employing and firing staff etc and has usually some political sway and respect in his town, I would rate his position still with 4 dots, while deputies may achieve a third dot if they have accumulated some experience and respect from collegues and the sheriff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11939-hardcorehannes","name":"HardcoreHannes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T04:39:23-08:00","id":1030520,"title":"","content":"Do not local Sheriffs respond to State Police? Because I know that State Police is <b>not</b> FBI, but it also have some very broad jurisdictions, often in the same places as FBI - well, only limited to one State territory, as name suggest.<br /> <br /> Because if Sheriffs are under State Police directly, then Status could seem like this:<br /> <br /> Status ( Local Police ):<br /> 1 dot - Deputy Sheriff<br /> 2 dot - Deputy Sheriff<br /> 3 dot - Local Sheriff<br /> 4 dot - State Police Detective<br /> 5 dot - State Police Chief","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T07:58:07-08:00","id":1030590,"title":"","content":"State police wouldn't be local police, they wouldn't be responding to something a local sheriff would, usually. And sheriffs don't 'respond to' them, their authority is the same, they just have different jurisdictions. State police would likely take charge of, or advise in, a matter that involved criminals who travel across many cities or counties. If something was a city or county only matter, state police would have no jurisdiction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T08:18:13-08:00","id":1030597,"title":"","content":"The role of sheriff/state police/local police depends very heavily on the state the game is taking place in for example in Pennsylvania<br /> <br /> \u200bMany small towns/rural areas rely on State Police as local law enforcement.<br /> <br /> \u200bSheriffs and deputies are the enforcement agents of the courts i.e. you owe fines so the court issues a warrant and the sheriff dispatches either constables or deputies to arrest you.<br /> <br /> \u200bMany suburbs contract police service from the city that they are affiliated with i.e. Lancaster City at one time provided Police services for Manor Township and Mannheim Township.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T08:22:32-08:00","id":1030598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lncbill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030343-status-police-and-usa-sheriffs?p=1030597#post1030597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The role of sheriff/state police/local police depends very heavily on the state the game is taking place in for example in Pennsylvania\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>This too. If you're having a game take place in the USA you could just look up how the area does things. Alaska doesn't have sheriffs or county police, for instance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T11:08:03-08:00","id":1030678,"title":"","content":"You're not really getting it right, so I will try to clarify using the usual case in the US.<br /> <br /> The FBI, DEA, ATF, and other &quot;men of letters&quot;, also referred to collectively as &quot;Feds&quot;, have power everywhere in the US but only deal with <b>national</b> laws. The state police have power everywhere within their state and only deal with <b>state</b> laws which include murder, rape, robbery, and the usual crimes of modern societies. However, the state police give up power to sheriffs and police in most areas when they exist. State police typically patrol highways and state parks. A city should have worked out a contract to employ a police force so their police have power within the city. Outside of such cities, a sheriff has power for a whole county. A sheriff is an elected official. A police chief is typically appointed by a mayor or city council which are elected officials. In all cases, the state police train sheriffs and police unless there is an academy in the area.<br /> <br /> This effectively means that within a city the police have power, outside a city the sheriff has power except for state parks and highways where the state police have power unless someone commits a federal crime then the Feds come in. Some places will not have police and/or sheriffs so you keep moving right in that case. Example:<br /> <br /> If you commit murder in a city the police will investigate. If you commit the murder outside of the city then the sheriff will investigate. However, if you commit the murder on the highway even within a city then the state police will investigate. If you commit murders in multiple states then the FBI will investigate. If you commit 100 murders within a single state across multiple cities, counties, highways and parks then you will have multiple sheriffs, multiple police forces, and the state police investigating. None of them have to help each other but common sense dictates they would. The FBI could do nothing about it. The counter to that is the police, sheriffs, and state police can always ask for help from other law enforcement agencies.<br /> <br /> If you mean Status (law enforcement) then the Feds would be at the top, the state police would be next to them, sheriffs would be in the middle and deputies and police would be on the lower end. If the Status is defined to the local areas then the the police chief would be at the top for the city and the sheriff would be at the top outside of the city. This is assuming an urban area. Rural areas would not have police. Wilderness might not have sheriffs either, but they might have police for a city trapped in the wild.<br /> <br /> If you are within a large city then I would exclude everyone but police for simplicity. The fact that it would be sheriff's deputies delivering court orders is not worth bringing into the rules. If the city is smaller, under a million, then put the sheriff at the top and the police chief under the sheriff. Forget the state police as they are largely irrelevant unless there is no police or sheriff around. You would not have police for rural areas so the sheriff is still at the top but no police chief. Sheriff's departments have ranks just like the police do with captains, lieutenants, sergeants, and detectives.<br /> <br /> County<br /> Sheriff<br /> Ranked (sergeant, etc.) Deputy<br /> Detective<br /> Deputy<br /> <br /> City<br /> Police Chief<br /> Captain<br /> Lieutenant/Sergeant<br /> Detective<br /> Patrol officer<br /> <br /> Hybrid (maybe) for medium population area<br /> Sheriff<br /> Police Chief<br /> Ranked officers/deputies<br /> Detectives<br /> Patrol officers and deputies<br /> <br /> or<br /> <br /> Sheriff/Police Chief<br /> High rank officers/deputies<br /> Low rank officers/deputies<br /> Detectives<br /> Patrol officers and deputies <br /> <br /> If the setting was for an entire state and you want a player to have such status then that would be a mess. See example above.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T11:27:14-08:00","id":1030690,"title":"","content":"Thanks, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1526\" data-vbnamecard=\"1526\">Diggs</a>, it really showed me how this would be complex problem...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:01:37-08:00","id":1030739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030343-status-police-and-usa-sheriffs?p=1030690#post1030690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1526\" data-vbnamecard=\"1526\">Diggs</a>, it really showed me how this would be complex problem...</div> </div> </div> </div>You're welcome. There are many different status merits across books, but the CoD has Status (police) and it is indicated that the merit is for a single organization so stick with simplicity. Status (police) for city. Status (sheriff) for county outside of city. State police are usually a niche group inappropriate for a game or replace (sheriff). Feds may or not be appropriate depending on specific setting but they too would be a separate Status, for each agency.<br /> <br /> I don't agree with informant being on the list though because I've never heard of them having access to facilities. Law enforcement is pretty powerful, typically.<br /> <br /> 5- Head of force (chief)<br /> 4- High Level Administrator (precinct commander)<br /> 3- Administrator of a small group (head of robbery or homicide, etc.)<br /> 2- Detective<br /> 1- Patrol<br /> <br /> I think that is better. Then you have separate status for city, county and region (feds). Names are less important if you aren't sure about the specific State, etc. I think that will suffice for most games in the US.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:09:05-08:00","id":1030743,"title":"","content":"Thank you very much, as I'm running games in Poland and we like to play in 'Hollywood USA' so some oversimplifications are okay, but more I know about real life USA, more material we have to play. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:18:13-08:00","id":1030747,"title":"","content":"I can back up what Diggs said.<br /> That's pretty much how it works in Washington State for example. I grew up outside of a city so the Sheriff's Office (meaning deputies) were the only police I dealt with at home, but if I went in to town it was that town's police, and on the state highways it was the State Patrol. The State Parks also has a Ranger service that is separate from the State Patrol.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T14:39:00-08:00","id":1030801,"title":"","content":"I came up with this for a couple games I've been in. That said, Diggs' 1-5 summary is pretty awesome too. Membership in different police organizations will be more or less useful in some games than others.<br /> <br /> Status: County Police<br /> 1: Deputy<br /> 2: Lieutenant<br /> 3: Captain<br /> 4: Undersheriff<br /> 5: Sheriff<br /> <br /> Status: FBI<br /> 1: Probationary agent<br /> 2: Special Agent<br /> 3: Senior Special Agent or Supervisory Special Agent<br /> 4: Assistant Special Agent in Charge (ASAC)<br /> 5: Special Agent in Charge (SAC)<br /> <br /> Status: Municipal Police<br /> 1: Beat cop<br /> 2: Detective or sergeant<br /> 3: Lieutenant or captain<br /> 4: Commander or deputy chief<br /> 5: Chief of police<br /> <br /> Status: State Police<br /> 1: Trooper<br /> 2: Sergeant<br /> 3: Lieutenant or captain<br /> 4: Major or lieutenant colonel<br /> 5: Colonel<br /> <br /> Status: U.S. Marshals<br /> 2: Deputy United States Marshal<br /> 3: Inspector or Supervisory Deputy United States Marshal<br /> 4: Chief Deputy United States Marshal<br /> 5: United States Marshal","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T15:01:20-08:00","id":1030815,"title":"","content":"Diggs described the basic arrangement pretty well. It can actually get pretty complicated depending where you live. I live in the Florida state capital and there are easily over a dozen different law enforcement agencies operating here with varying jurisdictions. For example, there is the city police and the county sheriff that work basically like Diggs described. Then there is Florida State University and FAMU that have their own police departments with enforcement powers on their respective campuses. There are the capitol police who have jurisdiction over the capitol buildings. There is the Florida Highway Patrol and various other state level enforcement agencies with their own jurisdictions (Fish and Wildlife; Agricultural Law Enforcement; Alcoholic Beverages and Tobacco). There are even some school districts (K-12) in Florida that are big enough to have their own police separate from their city or county police.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1039-brennanhawkwood","name":"Brennanhawkwood","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T18:24:18-08:00","id":1030911,"title":"","content":"I thought about university police but thought it would be confusing, but if the hamster wants more info then I can oblige. There are multiple Hollywoods in the US. I assume this is Hollywood, California. That would be a neighborhood in Los Angeles so the local law enforcement would be the Los Angeles Police Department, third largest in the country. They would investigate crimes. I would put the person in charge of Hollywood at Status 4. The Los Angeles Sheriff's Department would deliver court orders and manage the jails. Jails are usually run by the sheriff instead of the police which only hold people temporarily.<br /> <br /> There probably are campus police for each university in the area which can kick people off the campus or issue tickets but not much else unless somebody gets violent. The highway patrol monitor the highways for traffic law violations. Game wardens monitor state parks for violations. The Department of Corrections manages state prisons. While the highway patrol, game wardens, and prison guards are all technically state police the highway patrol would have the detectives investigating crimes except possibly for the prisons. The DoC has investigators too.<br /> <br /> The US has state prisons and federal prisons so if someone breaks out of a federal prison then the US marshals would hunt them down. They also transport federal prisoners or oversee the transfers at least. For the state prisons it would be the DoC that transports and tracks escaped prisoners. The highway patrol would likely be arresting the escaped prisoners though.<br /> <br /> The last two paragraphs describe where I live which is not California but it looks like California has the same setup. Many states work that way so you could use that as standard for the US and be okay.<br /> <br /> BTW if you are confused, the law enforcement officers get confused too. It is not uncommon for nobody to know what the hell they are supposed to do because of all the jurisdictions. They also get annoyed when they can't do anything because of jurisdictions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1526,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","user":"Diggs","date":". 01-12-2017, 09:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T18:39:36-08:00","id":1030922,"title":"","content":"Don't mistake Status with Authority or Jurisdiction, it can and often is totally unrelated.<br /> \u200bAuthority is the legal right to act in the name of another, be it an individual, organization or even government.<br /> \u200bJurisdiction is stewardship and responsibility for, in the case of police, law enforcement in a defined region.<br /> \u200bStatus on the other hand is the measurement of respect and good will within that group.<br /> It is totally possible for the office holder with the highest authority and largest jurisdiction in the land to have Status (Police) 0, while the little old man who empties the office garbage cans has Status (Police) 5.<br /> \u200bThe result is that when mister high and mighty is in an officer's face it is all yes sir, you're right sir and how high sir? Then when his back is turned said officer gives him the finger, makes disparaging speculative remarks about the man's parentage and promptly ignores whatever he can get away with. If Mister Status 0 ever finds himself at the mercy of officers who aren't under his authority he is no better off than anyone else with Status 0, he best be polite or they might ream him as hard as they can.<br /> \u200bMeanwhile Mister Authority 0 but Status 5 is greeted at the office with a friendly hello, if he ever finds himself on the wrong side of police attention he gets a pat on the back with a &quot;don't worry about it&quot; and if he ever needs help he gets prompt service with a smile (a sincere smile as they want to help him). If Mister Status 5 finds himself on the wrong side of the law in another Jurisdiction where his Status doesn't directly apply, one of the calls made is to a friendly officer back home who replies with an &quot;I'll do what I can&quot; as said officer uses whatever Status he has to help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T02:25:34-08:00","id":1031058,"title":"","content":"Status isn't chain of command, everyone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T07:59:43-08:00","id":1031158,"title":"","content":"I forgot about campus police, we have those too. AND I forgot about tribal police, the Reservations have their own forces that are overlapped with the city and county jurisdictions<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lnodiv</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030343-status-police-and-usa-sheriffs?p=1031058#post1031058\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Status isn't chain of command, everyone.</div> </div> </div> </div>It isn't necessarily the same, but it is a reasonable starting point. Individuals can buck the trend as 2ptTakrill said, but generally higher in the organization would mean higher Status (police or not).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T10:32:02-08:00","id":1031237,"title":"","content":"Actually, sometimes status is the chain of command. The guy in charge of the precinct is going to start out with a high level of status. He could lose it later on and still be in charge but he can't lose much or he will be transferred, fired, or forced to retire. There is some flexibility, but losing all status or never having any requires a very good explanation. This is not always the case for every kind of group, but it is for groups that have a literal chain of command. When you have rank, you will have the benefits of Status. The merit's text covers the flexibility within it. We can only offer what a typical Status would be in a particular situation, which is what everyone has tried to do.<br /> <br /> If it was unclear then I hope this clarifies the situation. When talking about authority or jurisdiction, I believe everyone has meant that the Status works with the organization there. It would not in fact be Status (police) because there is no such organization, but there would be for example Status (LAPD). That gets you far in Los Angeles, but in Chicago it counts for nothing. Any benefits of Status would be a courtesy which would be reflected by a bonus to convincing someone to allow you the benefit, rather than an assumed benefit.<br /> <br /> Trying to use your Status outside of your jurisdiction would get you a warning, then a threat, and if you still didn't get the message, arrested. Law enforcement is territorial, presumably more so in Darkness. Speaking about the US still. It would be like Status (corporation). It would be for a specific corporation and meaningless for a different corporation. This is why jurisdiction has been brought up because not everyone does it that way and there was clearly confusion by the OP in the beginning. Most of the information provided is to indicate the boundaries of the organization as a whole. Ranks to dots is a general guideline, same case as provided by the merit.<br /> <br /> I imagine some people scratch their head when someone says the Highway Patrol has to be called for an accident on the highway in the city even if the Police witnessed the whole thing and vice versa if the accident occurs right off the highway on a connecting street. By highway, I mean what looks like a normal street in the city too, not like the Autobahn.<br /> <br /> Not at all disagreeing with Status still being useful outside of jurisdiction as long as it is tied back to the organization within jurisdiction or how the merit works in general. Good points.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T10:46:01-08:00","id":1031242,"title":"","content":"There is one situation were someone can rise as high as a city commissioner without having any status in the local police organization, a situation that may arise frequently in a world of darkness, it's called corruption. The person in question was given his position as a puppet for someone with the power and desire to do so, his 0 Status would in part be a side effect of everyone knowing it.<br /> \u200bThink Roscoe and Boss Hog.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}